strated the entire  tragedy just to lure danny into the cult  but writer director ari astor says that  this explanation doesn't hold up  telling the huffington post i can go as  far as to say that it's totally  incorrect he is not responsible  however he did state that the flowers  were an important symbol  explaining it was our way of weaving in  the idea that whatever is inevitable  is bound to happen although danny's  parents barely appear in midsummer  there's a brilliant little detail  included in the moment where we see the  doomed couple in bed  hello you have reached the ardor  residence  when their answering machine picks up  danny's call you can hear their recorded  message state that their last name is  arder  why is this significant as it turns out  the latin word arter loosely translates  to heat or to  burn not only are danny's travel  companions burned in the sacrificial  fire in the final scenes of the film  this fire also represents a sort of  spiritual rebirth for dany  whether she descends into madness or  finally experiences true catharsis  after grieving there's no denying that  by the end she has gone through a  dramatic change  it seems like astor chose this  particular name to emphasize that dany's  path has been set from the start  in some sense even before she met pel  and went to sweden it seems that danny  was always destined to be the may queen  and join the hauraga  despite the flames it's chilling to  think about  while watching midsummer for the first  time it's easy to blink and miss  all of the clues and symbols hidden  within the background artwork  but upon your second viewing look  closely when it comes to ari astor's  sets there are no accidents and all of  the artwork reveals hidden details about  the horror that's going to unfold  after witnessing what happened to dany's  family we see how her life plays out in  the aftermath  she spends much of her time isolated and  sleeping in her apartment  in one scene she's shown napping on her  couch right underneath a painting that  actually gives us a peek at what's  coming later  in the painting a girl wearing a crown  is kissing a brown bear on the nose  this symbolizes danny and christian's  relationship and if you've already seen  christian meet his end in the bear suit  there's no question as to why this  painting was chosen  and similar imagery warns christian of  what's ahead for him shortly before he  is sacrificed  while he's sitting in siv's house he's  staring directly at an image of a bear  on fire  when dany's friends are killed by the  haurga their bodies are stuffed and  dressed like scarecrows before they're  burned  it's an unsettling sight but even in the  early scenes of the film there are a few  very subtle hints that point toward  these events  for example when danny and christian are  hanging out with christian's friends  before their trip to sweden  a framed photo of the scarecrow from the  wizard of oz is visible  on top of a refrigerator in the  apartment there's also a stuffed  scarecrow doll sitting on top of a shelf  speaking with vulture writer director  ari astor said that his film was quote  the wizard of oz for perverts and during  a conversation with the nerdist the  director said that he noticed mostly in  retrospect quite a few parallels between  his movie  and the judy garland classic both are  stories of young women who travel to a  strange new world along with several  bumbling sidekicks and find themselves  during the journey  plus the color scheme even changes when  dany shows up in sweden  switching from darker tones to much  brighter hues  when dany expresses her distress over  watching the gruesome etestupa ceremony  and begins packing up to leave  pel steps in and tells her that he  understands why it was so upsetting for  her  he reveals that he also struggled with  grief after his parents died in a fire  the first time you see midsummer it's  easy to assume that pell does genuinely  relate to what dany's going through  clearly he lost his parents in a tragic  accident too and he's just trying to  empathize with her right  but when you watch this particular  conversation with the knowledge of the  hourga's traditions of fiery human  sacrifices it opens up new possibilities  was pell telling a half-truth when he  talked to danny about his past  perhaps his parents really did die in a  fire but what if their death was part of  a sacrificial ceremony like the one we  saw in the film  alternatively pell could have been  bluffing about his family history  entirely  after all he clearly knew what the rest  of his community had in store for his  friends and he was obviously attracted  to dany  maybe he was trying to exploit her  vulnerabilities and convince her that  she could trust him  either way the conversation takes on a  whole new tone  not long after arriving at the village  dany and her friends watch the hourga  children play a game they're unfamiliar  with  when they ask pell he tells them that  the game is called skin the fool  their friend simon jokes that it sounds  precious and the moment  is quickly forgotten it isn't until  later that we realize the significance  of this line  mark one of christian's friends doesn't  come across as particularly bright and  he's often the butt of the joke  it's no surprise that he's the first of  the group to disappear and for a while  viewers are left wondering what happened  to him it all makes sense when josh  sneaks out in the middle of the night to  secretly read the ruby rotter one of the  howard's sacred texts  he's shocked when mark shows up too but  upon closer inspection he realizes that  it's not mark at all  it's a villager by the name of ulf  wearing mark's face as a mask  as it turns out skin the fool wasn't  just a children's game  before committing suicide dany's sister  sends her a message saying that  everything feels  black she felt like there was no way out  from her suffering and upon her death  that 